Here is a complete, step-by-step guide to deploying your application on Render. You can perform these steps yourself to configure, commit, and launch the deployment.

Phase 1: Code Modifications
To prepare the application for Render, you need to edit two existing files and create two new ones.

Step 1. Update Backend CORS to support dynamic origins
Since the production frontend and backend will run on different domains, the Go backend needs to dynamically allow CORS requests from your production frontend URL.

Open 

expense-server/internal/middleware/cors.go
.
Change the allowedOrigins function to be exported as AllowedOrigins (capitalize the first letter) and add the Vite preview port http://localhost:4173 to the default list.
Update isAllowedOrigin to call the new capitalized function name:
Modified cors.go code snippet:

go
// AllowedOrigins reads origins from CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS (comma-separated)...
func AllowedOrigins() []string {
	if v := os.Getenv("CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS"); v != "" {
		parts := strings.Split(v, ",")
		for i := range parts {
			parts[i] = strings.TrimSpace(parts[i])
		}
		return parts
	}
	return []string{
		"http://localhost:5173",
		"http://localhost:4173", // Vite preview
		"http://127.0.0.1:5173",
	}
}
func isAllowedOrigin(origin string) bool {
	for _, o := range AllowedOrigins() {
		if o == origin {
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}
Next, open 

expense-server/cmd/server/main.go
.
Locate the CORS middleware configuration around line 90. Change AllowedOrigins to use your new exported function:
Modified main.go code snippet:

go
// 6. Add CORS so the frontend can reach the backend.
	c := cors.New(cors.Options{
		AllowedOrigins: middleware.AllowedOrigins(),
		AllowedMethods: []string{"GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS"},
		AllowedHeaders: []string{
			"Authorization",
			"Content-Type",
			"Connect-Protocol-Version",
			"Connect-Timeout-Ms",
			"Connect-Accept-Encoding",
			"Connect-Content-Encoding",
			"Grpc-Timeout",
			"X-Grpc-Web",
		},
		AllowCredentials: true,
	})
Step 2. Create the Backend Dockerfile
Render works best and most reliably when we package the Go server inside a Docker container.

Create a new file named Dockerfile (no file extension) in the directory 

Expense_chatbot/expense-server/Dockerfile
 with the following content:

dockerfile
# Build stage
FROM golang:1.23-alpine AS builder
WORKDIR /app
# Copy dependency files first to cache dependencies
COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download
# Copy the rest of the source code
COPY . .
# Build the server binary as a statically compiled binary
RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -o server ./cmd/server
# Final runner stage
FROM alpine:latest
RUN apk --no-cache add ca-certificates tzdata
WORKDIR /app
# Copy the compiled binary and the migrations directory from the builder
COPY --from=builder /app/server .
COPY --from=builder /app/migrations ./migrations
# Expose the default backend port
EXPOSE 8080
# Run the server
CMD ["./server"]
Step 3. Create the Render Blueprint (render.yaml)
Render Blueprints let you configure and spin up your database, backend, and frontend at once.

Create a new file named render.yaml at the root of your workspace (

/e:/ExpenseTracker_Go/render.yaml
) with the following content:

yaml
services:
  # 1. Go Backend Web Service
  - type: web
    name: expense-tracker-backend
    env: docker
    dockerFilePath: Expense_chatbot/expense-server/Dockerfile
    dockerContext: Expense_chatbot/expense-server
    plan: free
    envVars:
      - key: APP_ENV
        value: production
      - key: PORT
        value: 8080
      - key: DATABASE_URL
        fromDatabase:
          name: expense-tracker-db
          property: connectionString
      - key: JWT_SECRET
        generateValue: true
      - key: CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS
        sync: false # You will enter the frontend static site URL here once it is deployed
  # 2. Svelte/Vite Frontend Static Site
  - type: static
    name: expense-tracker-frontend
    env: static
    buildCommand: npm install && npm run build
    publishPath: dist
    rootDirectory: Expense_chatbot/Frontend
    envVars:
      - key: VITE_API_URL
        sync: false # You will enter the backend's URL here once it is deployed
databases:
  # 3. Managed PostgreSQL Database
  - name: expense-tracker-db
    plan: free
Phase 2: Commit and Push to Git
Open your terminal at the root of the project (e:\ExpenseTracker_Go) and commit these changes:

bash
# Add the modified and newly created files
git add .
# Commit changes
git commit -m "chore: configure project for Render deployment"
# Push changes to your remote GitHub/GitLab repository
git push origin main
Phase 3: Setup on Render
Log in to dashboard.render.com.
Click New + in the top right corner and select Blueprint.
Connect your GitHub/GitLab repository.
Render will read the render.yaml file and show the services it plans to create:
expense-tracker-db (Database)
expense-tracker-backend (Web Service)
expense-tracker-frontend (Static Site)
Under the Service Parameters prompt, Render will ask you to input values for the two environment variables marked with sync: false:
VITE_API_URL: Enter a placeholder URL for now (e.g. https://expense-tracker-backend.onrender.com).
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS: Enter a placeholder URL for now (e.g. https://expense-tracker-frontend.onrender.com).
Click Apply. Render will start building and spinning up the PostgreSQL database and both services.
Phase 4: Finalize URLs (Syncing CORS & Frontend)
Since Render might append a random suffix to your service names (e.g., expense-tracker-backend-xyz.onrender.com), you need to update the environment variables with the actual live URLs:

Once the backend and frontend services have finished deploying, look at your Render Dashboard to find their actual URLs:
Frontend URL (looks like https://expense-tracker-frontend-xxxx.onrender.com)
Backend URL (looks like https://expense-tracker-backend-xxxx.onrender.com)
Go to the expense-tracker-frontend service in Render:
Navigate to Environment.
Update VITE_API_URL to the exact URL of your deployed backend.
Save changes. This will automatically trigger a new frontend build with the correct backend address.
Go to the expense-tracker-backend service in Render:
Navigate to Environment.
Update CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS to the exact URL of your deployed frontend.
Save changes. The backend will restart and begin accepting requests from the frontend.
You are all set! Your application will be live and fully connected.

